# Event-history retention and bounded cleanup

The existing `app.tasks.maintenance.cleanup_old_events` task retains 90 days by
default. Its daily 03:00 UTC beat schedule is unchanged. The LogDB migration also
installs a separate 180-day Timescale chunk retention policy and compression
after 14 days. These are separate mechanisms: 180 days is not a promise that the
manual row cleanup keeps that much history. This change does not shorten either
existing policy or add deletion of report-delivery history.

Each cleanup transaction selects at most 1,000 expired rows, using the complete
(id, timestamp) primary key, an explicitly materialized batch and SKIP LOCKED.
Each transaction commits independently. A sweep stops after 100 batches or a
200-second work budget; individual statements have a 10-second timeout and lock
waits a one-second timeout. Database errors propagate as task failures. Celery's
existing retry policy may start another bounded sweep; earlier commits survive.
The work budget is checked between batches, not a strict whole-process deadline.

The returned result includes `deleted`, `batches`, `remaining_expired` and
`complete`. `success=true, complete=false` means the sweep ran successfully but
expired data still exists, including rows skipped because another transaction
locked them. The worker logs a warning for that result. It never performs an
unbounded final delete to force completeness, and it does not count the whole
backlog before starting. Completion is a point-in-time observation, not a
guarantee against later inserts with older timestamps.

Invalid days (including zero, negative values, booleans and non-integers) are
refused before opening a database session. Valid values are 1..36500 days.

## Operations and capacity

Review incomplete results and warnings, active locks, database disk space,
autovacuum and Timescale job failures. A row cap does not bound all physical I/O:
compressed chunks can require decompression, and deleted rows do not immediately
return disk space to the OS. The default daily row-sweep cap is 100,000 rows; do
not assume it can keep up with a larger steady expiration rate. Timescale chunk
retention remains independent. A growing backlog needs measured sizing and an
operator-reviewed schedule/capacity policy, not an unbounded DELETE or a full
disk on the production host. Never run VACUUM FULL or drop chunks as an automatic
reaction to an incomplete sweep.

## Reproducible acceptance

From a reviewed public source tree on a Linux Docker host with the candidate
production backend and LogDB images already present:

```sh
python3 scripts/check_event_retention.py \
  --backend <candidate-backend-image-or-id> \
  --logdb <candidate-logdb-image-or-id> \
  --work-dir /private/new-retention-fixture
```

The tool resolves local image IDs without pulling and creates an internal
network, a fresh tmpfs database and restricted application container. It exposes
no host ports, accepts no existing volumes/database URLs and does not contact
hardware. It runs the shipped LogDB migration, then checks an empty table,
the old oversized-delete negative control (rolled back), exact cutoff/composite
identity, concurrent sweepers, locked rows, compressed chunks, the default
100-batch cap and real mid-run SQL failure/recovery. Only `report.json` is designed
to share. The work directory contains generated credentials and raw failure
logs: keep it private. Cleanup verifies resource ownership labels before removal.

This is synthetic retention acceptance, not a production-sized disk-pressure or
sustained soak qualification. It does not cover report archives/delivery history,
all other telemetry tables, customer backup migration or multi-host availability.

PostgreSQL's DELETE documentation describes bounded deletion through a selected
batch: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-delete.html
